From dc37f26a5d6f9e9c0e9ee829247d8a0a3be26335 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 12:39:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Delay: clean repeats after time changes, cubic resampling,
 feedback filters; no crackle when saving

- After the delay time changes, the delay goes back to native (bit-exact)
  repeats. 1.2.1 refused a new buffer of the same size as the old one,
  which left it resampling for good after most changes, even the knob
  moved and back: every repeat lost 9 dB at 10 kHz.
- Resampled writing spreads each sample with a Catmull-Rom kernel at all
  speeds instead of triangles twice as wide as needed, and reading
  interpolates cubically. A modulated delay keeps its treble (0.6 dB at
  10 kHz per repeat instead of 9) and its grain drops from -59 to -80 dB;
  a 5 s delay keeps 5 kHz at -3 dB instead of -7.
- Speed and feedback glide within a block instead of stepping once per
  block (a buzz at 344 Hz). A new buffer glides along with the one it
  replaces, so a large jump of the delay time doesn't step when it takes
  over. All writing sits the same distance behind the read position
  (1.2.1 was one sample off in one path). The half-rate buffer is made
  only below 0.8x, so a modulated time doesn't keep changing buffers.
- Analog mode saturates with the antialiased tanh.
- New LPF and HPF in the feedback, each repeat darker or thinner than the
  last (Delay menu; off by default, saved only when on). In the digital
  mode the clipping comes after them, so the low cut's overshoot stays
  within the headroom; a low cut switched off releases its state.
- Filter and saturation states reset when the delay restarts; a copied
  delay keeps its sync type (1.2.1 dropped triplet and dotted).
- Reverb (from v10): the darkest damping of the Mutable and Digital models
  is saved as the darkest again on the Deluge. The firmware's
  -funsafe-math-optimizations turned max(1 - damping, 2^-31) into a
  comparison with 1 - 2^-31, which rounds to 1, so it was saved as 0 (no
  damping). Found by running the reverb test on the Cortex-A9 in an
  emulator.
- Saving no longer stalls the audio: while a song or preset is put
  together for the card, the audio routine runs with cluster loading, as
  it does while reading. 1.2.1 had that call commented out, a short buzz
  or crackle on every save.

